# Local Netlify folder
.netlify

# Universe source layer.
#
# COMMITTED: public/universe_candidates.json and public/universe_ranking.json
# are the artifacts the API serves; data/universe_active.json is the weekly
# rotation cache signal_engine consumes; data/pool/ holds the hand-maintained
# pool inputs (Nasdaq-100 membership, inclusions).
#
# COMMITTED as of 2026-08-10: data/universe_cache/etf_*.json. These are the
# only record of WHICH holdings produced a given pool. Without them, "SPY put
# NVDA in the pool" is an inference from whatever Yahoo returns today, not
# evidence about the build that actually ran. The rotation workflow commits
# them back on every rotation, so the committed copy is always the snapshot
# the build used — committing them without that step would have been worse
# than not committing them, since a permanently stale file reads as evidence.
#
# IGNORED, and never authoritative: the data/ copies of the ranking and
# candidates artifacts. They are local build byproducts of a local rotation
# run. Rotations normally run in Actions, which commits only the public/
# copies, so a data/ copy on a developer machine is frozen at whenever that
# machine last built and must not be read as current. Nothing in production
# reads them; nothing committed should either.
# NOTE: the first pattern must exclude the directory's CONTENTS (trailing
# /*), not the directory itself — git will not descend into an excluded
# directory, and the negation below would then never be reached. For the same
# reason the negation only works while the caches stay FLAT: an
# etf_*.json nested under a subdirectory here would be ignored again.
data/universe_cache/*
!data/universe_cache/etf_*.json
data/universe_candidates.json
data/universe_ranking.json

# Build 4 backtest — raw market-data pulls are cached locally, never
# committed; the committed artifacts are the results JSON + report + charts.
data/backtest_cache/
data/doctrine_cache/
